{"author":{"id":"f4e4dbe251fbd7519eab696986bae317e93a786739d6cebe9c4c1e89dd44e465"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793681561,"nonce":"t9ycDTWljczhASdjEaC396vrteI=","title":"Parent epic: `54c6e06f383039f9758f1571999c683db1c244561561998cf9a52c41b84fa52f`. Follows M4: `c6339e9e209d17cae689b2f9e65946055221923860c8f2b9083bb5d55116544a`.","message":"The kernel checks proofs against the Lean statements it is given. Whether those statements mean what the C# does depends on extraction and translation, `src/Warrant.Extraction` and `src/Warrant.Semantics`, about 4,100 lines. That code is trusted, and the kernel cannot catch mistakes in it.\n\nThe M4 code review demonstrated this. It found two constructs for which Warrant proved claims that are false on the CLR: a `[Closed]` class with a static constructor, and a record with its own copy constructor. The differential tests compare kernel evaluation with .NET only for corpus programs, so they missed both.\n\nWork:\n\n- A generator of random C# programs within the M4 subset: arithmetic and comparisons, locals, `if` and `switch` (statements and expressions), patterns, records, open and `[Closed]` enums, closed-set classes, and calls between verified methods.\n- For each generated program:\n  - compile it;\n  - run it with `Inputs.cs`'s boundary and random values;\n  - evaluate the translation in the kernel;\n  - compare the two results, as `tests/Warrant.Engine.Tests/Differential` already does for the corpus.\n- Shrink each failing program to a minimal repro.\n- Also generate near-miss programs, which the subset should reject, and check that extraction names each one with WRT0002 or WRT0009 rather than translating it.\n\nDone when the generator covers every VIR node kind and runs as a test with a fixed seed, plus a longer opt-in run.\n\nThe long-term alternative, recorded here so it is not lost, is a verified translator: a Lean semantics of the C# subset, and a proof that translation preserves it.","files":null}]}